  "rendered": "To calculate the diffusion time, we need to use the equation:\n\nt = l^2 / D\n\nwhere l is the diffusion length and D is the diffusion coefficient.\n\nGiven that the diffusion length (l) is varied from 5 nm to 50 nm, we will use l = 10 nm for the calculation.\n\nThe diffusion coefficient (D) for ZrO2 is given in Table 5 as 1.7 × 10^−18 cm^2/s.\n\nFirst, we need to convert the diffusion coefficient from cm^2/s to nm^2/ns. \n\n1 cm^2/s = 10^16 nm^2/ns\n\nSo,\n\nD = 1.7 × 10^−18 cm^2/s = 1.7 × 10^2 nm^2/ns\n\nNow we can plug in the values to the equation:\n\nt = (10 nm)^2 / (1.7 × 10^2 nm^2/ns)\nt = 100 nm^2 / (1.7 × 10^2 nm^2/ns)\nt = 0.588 ns\n\nTherefore, a lithium ion would take approximately 0.588 nanoseconds to cross a 10 nm ZrO2 coating.",
